Chapter: ProseAnchor
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Pins the prose-to-notation pairing. A prose function template names its
 function explicitly, therefore the consistency check must look that name
 up among every definition in the chapter rather than assuming the first
 one. Before this test existed the check compared every prose block
 against defs index zero, which meant a chapter with more than one
 definition produced a warning for each block after the first.

 The lexer half is pinned here too. A prose token used to be built after
 the lexer had already advanced its offset past the line, therefore each
 prose block captured the bytes following the line instead of the line
 itself. Corrupted prose classified as no template at all, which is why
 the pairing defect stayed invisible: the check never ran.

Section: Definitions

 To first-def (n) gives an Integer, doubling the given value.

  first-def : Integer -> Integer
  first-def (n) = n + n

 To second-def (n) gives an Integer, tripling the given value.

  second-def : Integer -> Integer
  second-def (n) = n * 3

 To third-def (n) gives an Integer, adding one to the given value.

  third-def : Integer -> Integer
  third-def (n) = n + 1

 To absent-def (n) gives an Integer.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("first=" & show (first-def 21))
    print-line-uni ("second=" & show (second-def 5))
    print-line-uni ("third=" & show (third-def 9))
  end
